20:25:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: In a first-person shooter (FPS), the exact method depends on whether the gun is *hitscan* (instant, like a laser) or *projectile-based* (travel time, like a rocket). But in both cases, the core challenge is keeping the experience fast and fair over the internet, where every player has a different latency (ping) to the server. Here’s a breakdown of how it works: --- ### 1. The two fundamental bullet types - **Hitscan (instant hit)** When you fire, the game instantly traces a line from your gun’s barrel into the world and immediately determines what it hits. Games like *Counter-Strike* and Valorant use this almost exclusively. *Real-time tracking:* there is no actual “flying” bullet object for hitscan weapons. The visual tracer you see is just a cosmetic effect; the damage is applied at the moment you click. - **Projectile (physical simulation)** The bullet is a moving object with speed, gravity, and sometimes drag. Games like Battlefield and Overwatch (e.g., Pharah rockets) simulate these physics step by step. *Real-time tracking:* The bullet’s position is updated every frame (or tick) on both your machine (the client) and the server. --- ### 2. The networking architecture Most modern FPS games use a **server-authoritative** model: - The game server runs the true simulation. It knows where every player actually is and handles all game rules (damage, death, scoring). - Your client (your PC/console) is just a “dumb terminal” that sends your inputs (aim direction, fire trigger) and receives snapshots of the world state. - To hide latency, the client runs its own local simulation that *predicts* what will happen before the server confirms it. --- ### 3. How the server tracks the bullet and decides hits #### A. For a **hitscan** shot: 1. **Client-side prediction** When you click fire, your client immediately draws a tracer, plays a sound, and shows a hit marker if it *thinks* you hit someone—based on your own view. But this is just a guess. 2. **Server-side lag compensation** Your “fire” command reaches the server a few dozen milliseconds later. The server now needs to check if you would have hit the target *at the moment you fired*. It can’t just use the current positions, because the target has moved since then. So the server rewinds every other player’s position to exactly where they were at your command’s timestamp (using a short history buffer). Then it performs a ray trace from your gun through your crosshair in that recreated past world. If the trace hits an enemy hitbox, it’s registered as a hit. This technique is called *backwards reconciliation* and is responsible for the “I shot him on my screen” fairness. 3. **Server confirmation** The server sends back a response: either damage applied (if hit) or nothing. Your client then updates the health bar, kill feed, etc. If your client predicted a hit wrongly, it corrects it (e.g., a “no-reg” effect if the target actually dodged). #### B. For a **projectile** shot: 1. **Spawn prediction** Your client spawns the bullet locally and starts moving it according to the same physics the server uses. This way you see the rocket fly immediately. 2. **Server-side authoritative simulation** The server receives your fire command and also spawns the projectile. From this point, the server simulates the bullet’s movement every tick (e.g., 60 or 128 times per second). It checks for collisions with player hitboxes or world geometry. Because projectiles have travel time, lag compensation is often more complex: some games rewind the target’s position to match the projectile’s arrival time, while others just use “favor the shooter” logic where if you hit on your screen, it counts (within a latency tolerance). 3. **Client-server sync** For network efficiency, the server periodically sends the bullet’s position. Your client then smoothly interpolates (blends) between its own predicted position and the server’s authority to correct any drift. If the server says the rocket missed but your client shows an explosion, the explosion effect gets instantly removed—a “misfire” or visual correction. --- ### 4. Interaction with online players - **Seeing other players move** Your client doesn’t show raw positional data from the server—that would be jerky. Instead, it buffers a few snapshots and interpolates between them, so enemy movement appears smooth even if you only get updates every 50 ms. - **Hit registration feedback** When a bullet hits you, the server determines the damage, applies it, and broadcasts to both players. The shooter sees the hit marker, the victim sees blood/red screen. On low latency, this is nearly instant; on high latency, lag compensation makes it feel like your shot counted when it should have. - **Anti-cheat and verification** All hit logic runs on the server to prevent clients from claiming impossible shots. The server validates shot angles, projectile speeds, and whether the target was actually in line-of-sight. --- ### In short: The game doesn’t “track the bullet” in a vacuum. It’s a careful dance between: - **Your local prediction** (so it feels crisp) - **The server’s authoritative simulation** (for fairness) - **Lag compensation** (to honor what you saw on your screen within a reasonable time window) - **Rapid state updates and interpolation** (to show you where everyone else is) The result is a seamless illusion: you pull the trigger, the server determines the outcome using smart time-travel trickery, and everyone sees a consistent—if slightly delayed—version of the same gunfight.
